<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleusinian Mysteries</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eleusinian Mysteries were the secret initiation rites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> and her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>, or Kore, held each year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> under the supervision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Initiates walked in procession from Athens along the Sacred Way and were promised a better fate after death. The rites drew people from across the Greek and Roman world until the sanctuary was abandoned at the end of the fourth century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries.jpg" alt="Eleusinian Mysteries" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marsyas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Myst%C3%A8res_d%27Eleusis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mysteries had a basis in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> agrarian cult, and some of their practices seem to have been influenced by the religion of the Mycenaean period. Excavations have shown a private building under the Telesterion in Mycenaean times, so the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> may originally have been private. They were tied to the myth in the Homeric Hymn to Demeter, from about 650 BCE. In it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> is seized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>, and Demeter searches for her and causes a drought. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> lets the daughter return, but she has eaten pomegranate seeds in the underworld and must spend part of each year there.</p><p>Under Peisistratos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> the Mysteries became pan-Hellenic, and about 300 BCE the state took over their control, which was held by two families, the Eumolpidae and the Kerykes. The only requirements were freedom from blood guilt and the ability to speak Greek, and men, women and slaves could be initiated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Lesser Mysteries were held at Agrai in the month of Anthesterion, around February or March. Participants sacrificed a piglet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> and purified themselves in the river Ilisos, and became initiates, or mystae. The Greater Mysteries were held in Boedromion, around September or October, and lasted ten days. On the 18th of the month the procession left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerameikos">Kerameikos</a> and walked along the Sacred Way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a>, calling Íakche. At Eleusis there was an all-night vigil, and initiates drank the kykeon, a drink of barley and pennyroyal.</p><p>On the 19th the initiates entered the Telesterion, where it is widely supposed that the rites had three elements: things done, a dramatic reenactment of the myth; things shown, a display of sacred objects by the hierophant; and things said. The penalty for divulging them was death, which is probably why almost nothing is known of them. Hippolytus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> wrote that the highest grade was shown an ear of grain reaped in silence. Initiates believed that they would have a better fate in the underworld.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Some scholars hold that the kykeon was a psychoactive drink, a hypothesis that continues to receive attention but was not well received by classicists. In 170 the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> was sacked by the Sarmatians and rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>. Julian was the last emperor to be initiated. Eunapius reports that the Mysteries were closed in 392 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, and that in 396 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaric-i">Alaric I</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, looted the remains of the shrines. According to the historian Hans Kloft, elements of the cult survived in the Greek countryside, where Demeter's duties passed partly to Saint Demetrius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleusinian Mysteries” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religious life centred on Athena Polias, honoured every four years in the Great Panathenaia, and on festivals such as the City Dionysia, where tragedies and comedies were staged, and the <b>Eleusinian Mysteries of Demeter</b> and Kore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet the killing of a slave was punished, and slaves took part in some festivals and the <b>Eleusinian Mysteries</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
